@ 46.5 - Old English-to-Scots Vowel Shifts
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Phonological history of Scots”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Phonological_history_of_Scots>; and Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Scottish Vowel Length Rule”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Scottish_vowel_length_rule> Application of the Scottish vowel-length rule:
	ai > ɛi / when stem-final
	ɛi > əi / when stem-final
	u:[+long] > ʌu / when-stem final, in northern varieties
	ø:[+long] > wi / {k,ɡ}_ (in Mid Northern dialects)
	ø:[+long] > i (in northern dialects)
	ø:[+long] > (j){u,ʌ} / _{k,x} (outcome varies depending upon dialect)
	a > i / _n (in northern varieties)
	a > e / _n (otherwise)
	a > {ɛ,e} / _rC
	ai oi ui ei au ou iu ɛ(o)u > e:[+long] oe əi i:[+long] {ɑ:[+long],ɔ:[+long]} ʌu ju j(ʌ)u
	ɛ:[+long] > ɛi ( / in some northern varieties
	ɛi ( > əi?) / in some northern varieties
	i:[+long] e:[+long] ɛ:[+long] a:[+long] o:[+long] u:[+long] {ø:[+long],y:[+long]} > əi i {i,e} e o u ø
	æ > ɛ / _C:[+alveolar]
	a ɔ u > {a,ɑ} ɔ ʌ
	— V > V:[+long] / _{r,F:[+voice],$,#}
	— əi > aɪ / _{r,F:[+voice],$,#} (pursuant to the above)